{"name":"Warm Wassail","hex":"#a66e68","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"warm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a somewhat high temperature, often but not always connoting that the high temperature is pleasant rather than uncomfortable.","Friendly and with affection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make or keep warm.","To become warm, to heat up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/warm"},{"term":"wassail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A toast to health, usually on a festive occasion.","The beverage served during a wassail, especially one made of ale or wine flavoured with spices, sugar, roasted apples, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To toast, to drink to the health of another.","To drink wassail."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wassail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wassail","blocks":[{"text":"The tradition of wassailing falls into two distinct categories: the house-visiting wassail and the orchard-visiting wassail. The house-visiting wassail, which traditionally occurs on the twelfth day of Christmastide known as Twelfth Night or Epiphany Eve, is the practice of people going door-to-door, singing and offering a drink from the wassail bowl in exchange for gifts; this practice still exists, but has largely been displaced by carol singing. The orchard-visiting wassail is the custom of visiting orchards in cider-producing regions of England and singing to the trees to promote a good harvest for the coming year. Notable traditional wassailing songs include \"Here We Come a-Wassailing\", \"Gloucestershire Wassail\", and \"Gower Wassail\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Shirehampton_wassail_music.jpg/330px-Shirehampton_wassail_music.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wassail","wikidata":"Q3405739"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg/500px-Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg/1280px-Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg/500px-Wassail_%284221293123%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pot of simmering wassail, infused with citrus fruit slices and cinnamon sticks","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWassail_(4221293123).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Toast_tree_trolley.jpg/500px-Toast_tree_trolley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Toast_tree_trolley.jpg/1280px-Toast_tree_trolley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Toast_tree_trolley.jpg/500px-Toast_tree_trolley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An apple sapling, hung with toast, placed in a handcart and pushed around the streets during the Chepstow Mari Lwyd, 2014","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AToast_tree_trolley.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Old_Christmas%2C_Illustrated_London_News_24_Dec_1842.jpg/500px-Old_Christmas%2C_Illustrated_London_News_24_Dec_1842.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Old_Christmas%2C_Illustrated_London_News_24_Dec_1842.jpg/500px-Old_Christmas%2C_Illustrated_London_News_24_Dec_1842.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 